meta {
  name: Full Test Suite (JS File)
  type: http
  seq: 5
}

get {
  url: {{baseUrl}}/users
  body: none
  auth: none
}

assert {
  res.status: eq 200
  res.body[0]: isJson
  res.body: isArray
}

tests {
  //Add the path to the JS File: ./04-Tests and Assertions/Solution/userTests.js
  const { runUserTests } = require("");
  
  // Pass the response to this imported method
  runUserTests();
}

settings {
  encodeUrl: true
  timeout: 0
  followRedirects: true
  maxRedirects: 5
}

docs {
  # Full Test Suite (JS File) 📁
  
  This request runs the **exact same tests** as "Full Test Suite" — but all the logic lives in `userTests.js`.
  
  **The script tab is just 2 lines:**
  ```javascript
  const { runUserTests } = require("./userTests");
  runUserTests(res);
  ```
  
  **What `userTests.js` consolidates:**
  - ✅ Chai.js tests (status, array check, core fields)
  - ✅ Zod schema validation (full user object structure)
  - ✅ `UserSchema` export for reuse in other requests
  
  **Why use JS files?**
  - **Reusable** — import the same tests across multiple requests
  - **Clean** — request files stay minimal
  - **Maintainable** — update the schema once, applies everywhere
  
  **Compare:** Open "Full Test Suite" side-by-side to see the difference between inline and externalized tests.
}
